Context

Deutsche Bank has already invested in powerful GPU infrastructure, with dbLLM deploying open-weight AI models for internal teams and Ada providing AI asset discovery. Meanwhile, developers increasingly use AI-assisted tools such as GitHub Copilot and Gemini CLI, while more capable coding agents can now investigate defects, modify code, execute tests, and complete multi-step engineering tasks. Other large enterprises have demonstrated the value of internally operated AI infrastructure. BNP Paribas has established a shared GPU-backed model-serving platform within its own data centers, while LinkedIn uses internally served models across numerous production AI applications. These examples highlight the opportunity to move beyond simply hosting models toward making them accessible and useful across an organization.

Problem Statement

We want to investigate whether Deutsche Bank can use its existing GPU investments to build powerful, internally owned AI coding agents by combining open-weight models with an extensible harness such as Pi or OpenCode. The immediate challenge is to serve a capable coding model, integrate it with the harness, and demonstrate reliable end-to-end defect resolution—from investigating a bug and implementing a fix to preparing test data, executing sandboxed tests, and producing a draft pull request. We will compare accuracy, latency, developer effort, and cost against existing Gemini CLI and Copilot-assisted workflows, alongside a hypothetical Claude Code subscription cost scenario. Today, the performance and economics of this internal approach remain unvalidated. Proving its feasibility would establish a practical foundation for broader adoption of internally hosted models, potentially reducing external AI expenditure while improving control over sensitive data and building internal AI engineering expertise. In collaboration with dbLLM and Ada, this foundation could eventually make coding, OCR, embedding, vision, and reasoning models easier to integrate into everyday enterprise workflows.